['## Response to review feedback: legacy kind discriminator values

@holtskinner — regarding your comment *"The legacy examples shouldn't be changed"*: I investigated this thoroughly against primary sources and the original spec values were incorrect. Here is the evidence.

---

### Part kind values

The official `v0.3.0` tag at [`types/src/types.ts`](https://github.com/a2aproject/A2A/blob/v0.3.0/types/src/types.ts) defines the following (lines 741–810):

```ts
export interface TextPart extends PartBase {
  /** The type of this part, used as a discriminator. Always 'text'. */
  readonly kind: "text";
  ...
}

export interface FilePart extends PartBase {
  /** The type of this part, used as a discriminator. Always 'file'. */
  readonly kind: "file";
  file: FileWithBytes | FileWithUri;   // ← nested wrapper
  ...
}

export interface DataPart extends PartBase {
  /** The type of this part, used as a discriminator. Always 'data'. */
  readonly kind: "data";
  ...
}
```

This confirms:
- Kind values are **lowercase** (`"text"`, `"file"`, `"data"`), not PascalCase (`"TextPart"`, `"FilePart"`, `"DataPart"`)
- `FilePart` has a **nested `file` object wrapper**, not a flat structure

This is also confirmed in [`docs/whats-new-v1.md`](https://github.com/a2aproject/A2A/blob/main/docs/whats-new-v1.md) lines 307–330, which explicitly labels the following as **v0.3.0 Structure**:

```json
{ "kind": "text", "text": "Hello world" }
{ "kind": "file", "file": { "fileWithUri": "...", "mimeType": "..." } }
{ "kind": "data", "data": {"key": "value"} }
```

---

### Streaming event kind values

The same `v0.3.0` tag at [`types/src/types.ts`](https://github.com/a2aproject/A2A/blob/v0.3.0/types/src/types.ts) lines 524–548:

```ts
export interface TaskStatusUpdateEvent {
  /** The type of this event, used as a discriminator. Always 'status-update'. */
  readonly kind: "status-update";
  ...
}

export interface TaskArtifactUpdateEvent {
  /** The type of this event, used as a discriminator. Always 'artifact-update'. */
  readonly kind: "artifact-update";
  ...
}
```

The correct legacy values are **kebab-case** (`"status-update"`, `"artifact-update"`), not `"TaskStatusUpdateEvent"` or `"taskStatusUpdate"`.

---

### What was wrong before this PR

The pre-PR `main` branch had the following incorrect values in Appendix A.2.1:

| Type | Pre-PR (incorrect) | Correct (v0.3.0) |
|---|---|---|
| TextPart | `"kind": "TextPart"` | `"kind": "text"` |
| FilePart | flat structure, `"kind": "FilePart"` | `"kind": "file"` with nested `file` object |
| DataPart | `"kind": "DataPart"` | `"kind": "data"` |
| TaskStatusUpdateEvent | `"kind": "TaskStatusUpdateEvent"` | `"kind": "status-update"` |
| TaskArtifactUpdateEvent | `"kind": "TaskArtifactUpdateEvent"` | `"kind": "artifact-update"` |

The latest commit (`978e41a`) corrects all of these based on the official `v0.3.0` source.', '## Summary

The A2A Protocol Specification consistently uses `"kind"` as the discriminator field for Part types (TextPart, FilePart, DataPart), but **multiple documentation pages and tutorials incorrectly use `"type"`** instead. This creates confusion for implementers and breaks interoperability.

## Specification Reference

**Official A2A Protocol Specification:**
- URL: https://a2a-protocol.org/v0.2.6/specification/
- **Section: 6.5 - Part Types**

### Correct Field Name from Specification:

All Part types use `"kind"` as the discriminator:

**TextPart:**
```json
{
  "kind": "text",
  "text": "Why did the chicken cross the road? To get to the other side!"
}
```

**FilePart:**
```json
{
  "kind": "file",
  "file": {
    "name": "input_image.png",
    "mimeType": "image/png",
    "bytes": "iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAUA..."
  }
}
```

**DataPart:**
```json
{
  "kind": "data",
  "data": {
    "confirmationId": "XYZ123",
    "from": "JFK",
    "to": "LHR"
  }
}
```

## Documentation Pages with Errors

### 1. ❌ A2A Sample Methods and JSON Responses
**URL**: https://a2aprotocol.ai/docs/guide/a2a-sample-methods-and-json-responses

**Section**: Section 3 - Streaming Artifact Update Example

**Error Found:**
```json
{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "method": "artifact-update",
  "params": {
    "taskId": "task_123",
    "artifact": {
      "artifactId": "artifact_001",
      "name": "Generated Report",
      "parts": [
        {
          "type": "text",               // ❌ Should be "kind"
          "text": "<section 1...>"
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

**Correction Needed**: Change `"type": "text"` to `"kind": "text"` in all examples in Section 3.

---

### 2. ❌ Google A2A Python SDK Tutorial
**URL**: https://a2aprotocol.ai/docs/guide/google-a2a-python-sdk-tutorial

**Section**: "Sending a Non-Streaming Message"

**Error Found:**
```json
{
  "message": {
    "role": "user",
    "parts": [{"type": "text", "text": "how much is 10 USD in INR?"}],  // ❌ Should be "kind"
    "messageId": "uuid4().hex"
  }
}
```

**Correction Needed**: Change `"type": "text"` to `"kind": "text"` throughout the tutorial.

---

### 3. ❌ Python A2A Tutorial (May 2025 version)
**URL**: https://a2aprotocol.ai/docs/guide/python-a2a-tutorial-20250513

**Section**: "Understanding the Client Code" → "Sending a Non-Streaming Message"

**Error Found:**
```json
{
  "message": {
    "role": "user",
    "parts": [{"type": "text", "text": "how much is 10 USD in INR?"}],  // ❌ Should be "kind"
    "messageId": "uuid4().hex"
  }
}
```

**Correction Needed**: Update all Part examples to use `"kind"` instead of `"type"`.

---

### 4. ❌ Python A2A Tutorial (Archived Blog Post)
**URL**: https://a2aprotocol.ai/blog/python-a2a-tutorial

**Section**: Client code examples

**Error Found**: Multiple occurrences of `{"type": "text", "text": "..."}`

**Correction Needed**: Update to `{"kind": "text", "text": "..."}` or mark page as deprecated.

---

### 5. ❌ Google Codelabs Tutorial (External)
**URL**: https://codelabs.developers.google.com/intro-a2a-purchasing-concierge

**Section**: "The Communication Protocols"

**Error Found:**
```json
{
  "message": {
    "role": "user",
    "parts": [
      {"type": "text", "text": task}  // ❌ Should be "kind"
    ],
    "messageId": message_id,
    "contextId": session_id
  }
}
```

**Note**: This is Google's official tutorial, may need coordination with Google team.

---

### 6. ❌ agent2agent.info Community Documentation (External)
**URL**: https://agent2agent.info/docs/concepts/part/

**Section**: "Different Types of Parts"

**Error Found**: TypeScript interfaces using `"type"` as discriminator

**Note**: Community site, may need to notify site maintainers.

---

### 7. ❌ a2acn.com Chinese Documentation (External)
**URL**: https://a2acn.com/en/docs/concepts/part/

**Error Found**: Uses `"type"` as discriminator in examples

**Note**: International site, may need translation team coordination.

---

## SDK Implementations (All CORRECT ✅)

All official SDKs use `"kind"` correctly:

**Python SDK (a2a-python):**
```python
class TextPart(BaseModel):
    kind: Literal['text']
    text: str
```

**JavaScript SDK (a2a-js):**
```javascript
{ kind: 'text', text: 'Hello, world!' }
```

**.NET SDK (a2a-dotnet):**
```csharp
[JsonPropertyName("kind")]
public string Kind { get; set; } = "text";
```

## Impact

### 🔴 Confusion for Implementers
- **Specification says**: Use `"kind"`
- **Some tutorials show**: Use `"type"`
- Developers copy-paste examples and create non-compliant implementations

### 🔴 Interoperability Breaks
- Agents using `"kind"` (per spec) cannot parse messages with `"type"`
- Agents following documentation examples break spec compliance
- Creates fragmentation in A2A ecosystem

### 🔴 LLM Code Generation Issues
- Claude, GPT-4, and other LLMs see both `"kind"` and `"type"` in A2A materials
- Generate inconsistent code based on which examples they reference
- Perpetuates the inconsistency in generated implementations

## Recommended Actions

### For a2aprotocol.ai Documentation

1. **Audit All Pages** - Search all documentation pages for `"type": "text"` patterns
2. **Fix Identified Pages** - Update the 4+ pages listed above to use `"kind"`
3. **Add Linting** - Validate all JSON examples against protocol schema in CI/CD
4. **Issue Errata** - Publish errata noting the documentation error

### For External Sites

1. **Notify Google** - Contact Google Codelabs team about tutorial error
2. **Notify Community Sites** - Reach out to agent2agent.info and a2acn.com maintainers

## Additional Context

This issue was discovered while implementing A2A file attachment support in ConnectedBrain.DevUI.

The inconsistency affects:
- New implementers learning the protocol
- LLM-based code generation tools
- Cross-language interoperability
- Training materials and workshops

## Related Issues

- a2aproject/a2a-dotnet#269 - Field name violations (separate but related)

---

**Specification Reference**: https://a2a-protocol.org/v0.2.6/specification/ Section 6.5', '## Feature Proposal  (Superseded by this proposal https://github.com/a2aproject/A2A/issues/1255#issuecomment-3679633802)

Move the `mediaType` field from `FilePart` to the base `Part` object, allowing all part types to specify a media type.  Currently if you need to provide a media type, you have to send it as a `FilePart` which requires base64 encoding if you want to include the content inline.  base64 encoding is not particularly efficient and is not great for debugging.

## Current Structure

Currently, only `FilePart` has a `mediaType` field:

```protobuf
message Part {
  oneof part {
    string text = 1;
    FilePart file = 2;
    DataPart data = 3;
  }
}

message FilePart {
  string name = 1;
  string uri = 2;
  string mediaType = 3;  // Only available on FilePart
}

message DataPart {
  google.protobuf.struct data = 1;
}
```

## Proposed Structure

Move `mediaType` to the base `Part` object and make data an `any` type to hold structured data that conforms to the JSON object model:

```protobuf
message Part {
  string mediaType = 4;  // Available for all part types
  oneof part {
    string text = 1;
    FilePart file = 2;
    google.protobuf.any data = 3;
  }
}

message FilePart {
  string name = 1;
  string uri = 2;
  // mediaType removed from here
}
```

## Benefits

### 1. **TextPart with Media Type**
Text parts can specify their format explicitly:
- `text/plain` - Plain text
- `text/markdown` - Markdown formatted text
- `text/html` - HTML content
- `text/csv` - CSV data

```json
{
  "mediaType": "text/markdown",
  "text": "# Hello\n\nThis is **formatted** markdown."
}
```

### 2. **DataPart with Media Type**
Data parts can indicate their content type:
- `application/json` - JSON data
- `application/city+json`  - Standardized vocabularies based on JSON e.g. https://www.cityjson.org/specs/2.0.1/

```json
{
  "mediaType": "application/json",
  "data": {
       "x": 24,
       "y": 32
   }
}
```

### 3. **Consistent API**
All parts have the same interface for specifying content type, making it easier for:
- Clients to handle content type detection uniformly
- Agents to specify content types for any part
- Middleware to process or transform content based on type

### 4. **Better Content Negotiation**
Clients and agents can negotiate content types for all message parts, not just files:
- Request text in markdown vs plain text
- Specify preferred data serialization format
- Handle multiple representations of the same content

## Use Cases

### Use Case 1: Formatted Text Responses
```json
{
  "role": "agent",
  "parts": [
    {
      "mediaType": "text/markdown",
      "text": "Here's the analysis:\n\n## Results\n\n- Item 1\n- Item 2"
    }
  ]
}
```

### Use Case 2: Structured Data
```json
{
  "role": "agent",
  "parts": [
    {
      "mediaType": "application/city+json",
      "data": {
           "CityObjects": {
                "LondonTower": {
                    "type": "Bridge"
                }
          }
       }
    }
  ]
}
```

### Use Case 3: Mixed Content
```json
{
  "role": "agent",
  "parts": [
    {
      "mediaType": "text/html",
      "text": "<p>See the attached file:</p>"
    },
    {
      "mediaType": "image/png",
      "file": {
        "name": "chart.png",
        "uri": "https://example.com/chart.png"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```
See also #1088 for the recommendation to change `data` to any from struct.']

['**Problem:**

Clients retrieving task history via `tasks/get` with `historyLength` receive an array of `Message` objects. These `Message` objects lack timestamps, making it impossible for clients (especially those not using SSE or experiencing disconnects) to reliably render a chronologically accurate conversation log (e.g., a chat UI) with timings for each turn. The `Task.status.timestamp` only reflects the *latest* status change.

**Proposal:**

Introduce a new _optional_ parameter object to the `tasks/get` method: `statusHistoryRequest?: { since?: string; count?: number }`.

*   `since`: An optional ISO 8601 timestamp string. If provided, the server should return status history entries *equal to or newer than* this timestamp.
*   `count`: An optional integer. If provided, the server should limit the number of returned status history entries to the most recent `count` entries that meet the `since` criteria (if provided).

When a client includes the `statusHistoryRequest` parameter in a `tasks/get` request, the server should return a new optional field in the result: `statusHistory: TaskStatus[]`.

```typescript
// tasks/get request params modification
interface TaskGetParams {
  id: string;
  historyLength?: number; // Existing parameter
  statusHistoryRequest?: { // <<< NEW Optional Parameter Object
    since?: string;        // ISO8601 datetime string
    count?: number;        // Max number of *most recent* entries to return
  };
  metadata?: Record<string, any>;
}

// tasks/get response modification
interface TaskGetResponse { // Assuming this structure exists implicitly
  id: string;
  sessionId?: string;
  status: TaskStatus; // The current/latest status
  artifacts?: Artifact[];
  history?: Message[]; // Existing history (remains unchanged)
  statusHistory?: TaskStatus[]; // <<< NEW: Array of historical TaskStatus objects
  metadata?: Record<string, any>;
}
```

The `statusHistory` array would contain recorded `TaskStatus` updates for the task, **ordered chronologically (oldest first, newest last)**. This ordering is natural for displaying sequences of events over time.

*   If only `since` is provided, return all entries from that time forward, ordered oldest to newest.
*   If only `count` is provided, return the latest `count` entries, ordered oldest to newest.
*   If both `since` and `count` are provided, return the latest `count` entries *since* the specified timestamp, ordered oldest to newest.
*   If neither `since` nor `count` is provided within `statusHistoryRequest`, the server *may* choose a default behavior (e.g., return the latest entry, or a default count, ordered chronologically) or return an empty array/omit the field. Explicitly providing at least one is recommended for predictable results.

Each `TaskStatus` object includes the `state`, the `timestamp` of that status change, and potentially the `message` associated with that change. The `status` field at the top level of the response *always* reflects the absolute latest status of the task, regardless of the filters applied to `statusHistory`.

**Benefits:**

*   **Provides Timestamps:** Directly addresses the need for timestamps associated with historical events/messages.
*   **Chronological Ordering:** Returns history in a natural order for UI rendering (oldest first).
*   **Flexible Querying:** Allows clients to fetch history incrementally (`since`) or get recent context (`count`).
*   **Backwards Compatible:** Introduces a new parameter object and response field. Existing clients are unaffected. Servers only need to implement this if they support it.
*   **Leverages Existing Structures:** Reuses the defined `TaskStatus` interface.
*   **Alignment with Capability:** Provides a concrete and flexible mechanism for agents advertising `capabilities.stateTransitionHistory: true`.

**Example Request (Fetch since a specific time):**

```json
{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 1,
  "method":"tasks/get",
  "params": {
    "id": "de38c76d-d54c-436c-8b9f-4c2703648d64",
    "statusHistoryRequest": {
      "since": "2025-04-18T10:00:30Z"
    }
  }
}
```

**Example Request (Fetch latest 5 entries):**

```json
{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 2,
  "method":"tasks/get",
  "params": {
    "id": "de38c76d-d54c-436c-8b9f-4c2703648d64",
    "statusHistoryRequest": {
      "count": 5
    }
  }
}
```

**Example Response Snippet (Illustrative content based on request 1, showing oldest first):**

```js
{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 1, // Corresponds to the first example request
  "result": {
    "id": "de38c76d-d54c-436c-8b9f-4c2703648d64",
    // ... other fields ...
    "statusHistory": [ // Ordered oldest first
       {
        "state": "working",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-18T10:00:35Z" // Older entry >= 'since'
      },
      {
        "state": "input-required",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-18T10:01:00Z", // Newer entry >= 'since'
        "message": { "role": "agent", "parts": [...] }
      }
      // Potentially more entries... up to the current time
    ],
    "status": { // Current status *always* reflects the latest actual status
      "state": "input-required",
      "timestamp": "2025-04-18T10:01:00Z",
      "message": { "role": "agent", "parts": [...] }
    }
    // ... other fields like artifacts ...
  }
}
```

This approach offers enhanced flexibility for retrieving timed history while maintaining compatibility and leveraging existing protocol structures.', '## Description

The specification needs to clarify the agent's responsibilities and flexibility regarding including state messages in the Task message history.

## Questions to Address

1. **Agent discretion**: Are agents allowed to decide whether to include state messages (messages from `TaskStatus` objects) in the Task message history?

2. **Requirements**: Should state messages:
   - Always be included in the history?
   - Never be included in the history?
   - Be optional at the agent's discretion?

3. **Client awareness**: Do we need a mechanism to communicate to clients whether messages in the history originated from a `TaskStatus` object?
   - Should there be metadata or a field distinguishing state messages from regular messages?
   - How can clients determine the source of historical messages?

4. **Consistency**: Should this be left completely up to the agent implementation, or should the specification provide guidance for consistency across implementations?

## Context

Currently, there is ambiguity about:
- Whether agents should include task status messages in the message history
- How clients can distinguish between different types of messages in the history
- Whether this behavior should be standardized or left to agent implementation

## Impact

Clarifying this will:
- Help agent developers understand their responsibilities for maintaining message history
- Enable clients to properly interpret and display conversation history
- Improve interoperability between different A2A implementations
- Provide guidance for consistent behavior across agents

## Related Issues

- #1228 - State transition history capability
- #1158 - Agent capabilities and state transition history
- #194 - Is "history[]" really a "state transition history"?', '@yarolegovich 

> A client calls SendMessage and doesn't want history to be persisted at all.

I think there is some confusion as to what the history length parameter means for SendMessage.

According to the comments in the proto file, the history_length parameter  for MessageSendConfiguration is:

> // The maximum number of messages to include in the history. if 0, the
>  // history will be unlimited.
>  int32 history_length = 3;

According to the comments in the types.ts file:

> /** The number of most recent messages from the task's history to retrieve in the response. */
>   historyLength?: number;

Unfortunately, the specification document doesn't include the `MessageSendConfiguration` object definition at all, so these comments are all we have to go on.

I am assuming that comments in the proto file are just a poorly worded version of what is in the types.ts file.  However, the comment about the 0 value leaves me wondering how a user is supposed to say "don't send me any history values".

I think what you are proposing is that an unset history length would return all of the history items and a 0 value would return no history items.  

@kthota-g  @pstephengoogle   @mikeas1   Do you folks have any clarity on what was intended here?']

['Thanks for this proposal. The A2A team has been talking about extensions internally, so I'm glad to see others interested in this as well!

I agree with you on the benefits of adding extension declarations in an AgentCard. What I came up with is slightly different from what you've landed on, but compatible. I think there's a place for more than just adding new RPC methods via extensions, so I'm avoiding tying extensions too specifically to any particular purpose. I believe what I've come up with can still handle the case you're looking for, but it's going to shuffle some stuff around.

Here's what I've been thinking:

1. Allow AgentCards to declare extensions the agent supports.
2. Extensions are identified by a URI, ideally a URL pointing to documentation describing the extension specification. At this point, no specific form is required for the extension specification (i.e. it does not need to be a JSON document or something structured).
3. Declared extensions can have arbitrary configuration under a generic `params` field.
4. Declared extensions can be marked required, indicating the Agent will return errors if that extension is not followed by the client.
5. Request and response messages are updated to include a field indicating extensions present on the request/response.
6. Extensions that require additional fields to be passed in a protocol-defined request should place their data in the `metadata` fields of request messages. Extensions could also impose constraints on how messages should be structured (for example, how information should be structured in DataParts).

Something like this:
```typescript
interface AgentExtension {
  // The URI defining the profile.
  uri: string;
  // A short description of how this agent relates to the extension.
  description?: string;
  // Whether the Agent requires the client to use this extension.
  required?: bool;
  // Extension-specific configuration.
  params?: object;
}

// ...

// Example request message that would be updated
interface MessageSendParams {
  // Existing fields...
  // Extensions present on this request message. Value is the URI of the extension.
  extensions?: string[];
}
```

Here's how your example might look. The details of the methods would be left to the actual extension profile document.

```json
{

  "capabilities": {
    "extensions": [
      {
        "uri": "https://example.com/a2a/extensions/taskhistory/v1"
        "description": "Task history support",
        "params": {
          # The maximum duration a task is kept around for
          "expiration": "P7D"
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

Does that structuring work with what you'd like to use extensions for?', 'Overall your understanding is pretty much accurate. I'll address some comments inline below:

> While items (5) and (6) on your list suggest Ext-2.0 extensions can require modifying request/response format, I think you did not mean to limit extensions to such use cases. At least, it is unclear to me what it should cover and what its limitations might be. You may have intended to leave it vague intentionally to broaden the possible ways to extend the protocol?

You are correct that the proposal I have made is not limited to just modifying request/responses. Adding new methods, as you are intending with your proposal, is also supported. The details of what exactly an extension is intended to do are left to the extension profile specification (a document that defines what the extension is, how to implement it, and how it's used).

> Items (5) and (6) in your message suggest that (some) Ext-2.0 extensions can be invoked by the A2AServer when they are listed in the extensions prop of the requests. In this sense, they are more like server middleware (executed before and after the agent handler).

The client including `extensions` in a request is not required for an extension to be activated. But otherwise your assessment is correct: extensions as middleware is a very reasonable approach. However, it's not the only way. An extension could declare a separate endpoint that's used for processing extension-related requests, which would then be totally outside the standard A2A request path.

> Ext-1.0 proposes specific ways of adding custom APIs to A2A servers, enabling A2A libraries (e.g., the official A2A SDK) to implement a common logic for adding extension (e.g., def add_extension(...)). This allows for individual extension source code to be external to both A2A libraries and A2A agents. So, extensions can be easily shared and used as third-party dependencies.

I don't see anything in your proposal specific to the SDK side of this, but my thought is that the same logic applies: adding an extension should be as simple as calling `add_extension` on an A2A server. Extension developers can publish libraries that implement the logic for their extension, which should include simple ways of incorporating the extension logic (be it middleware or separate API routes) into both A2A servers and clients. There is an aspect of SDK design to this to allow easy extension hooks, which the A2A team has been discussing.

Addressing your specific questions:

> Does the AgentExtension.params prop include general or user-specific parameters? Or both?

I'm not entirely sure what you mean by user-specific parameters (perhaps you mean changing the content of `params` based on authentication provided in the HTTP request?), but I have been expecting extension configuration to be general. It's about providing information to clients that configures the way in which they interact with the server, via both requests and responses. To take your `TaskHistory` extension as an example, you could specify a separate root endpoint used for retrieving task history, which the client must then use when making TaskHistory requests, or you could specify the maximum time that a Task is kept in storage.

That said, the "extended" card could expose user-specific parameters, as the intention is that retrieving that card requires authentication. This allows the server to produce parameters specific to the caller.

> Can params be updated post-deployment? If yes (tying to the previous question), how can the client determine which of the parameters they are allowed to change?

This is a question that is applicable to all of the AgentCard, and the protocol does not dictate how AgentCards are versioned and changed. But, in general, I'd consider all parameters mutable, just as the rest of the AgentCard is mutable. We provide a free-form `version` field on the AgentCard, and I would recommend that implementers update that string when they make breaking changes.

> And following up on the previous one, if params are mutable, this means that any service that caches AgentCards (including A2A agents and registries) would have to set different TTLs for extensionful vs. extensionless agents. Or they would need to implement smarter caching logic. Maybe this is not a problem if, in the future, the A2A spec changes .well-known/agent.json to be dynamic (and all AgentCards are considered dynamic).

The protocol doesn't take a specific stance on the cacheability of AgentCards as a whole, but I'd recommend that implementers follow standard HTTP best practices: set cache control headers. If an agent developer wants to ensure that clients never cache their AgentCard, indicate that in a header.  I don't believe extensions meaningfully change this.', '@mikeas1 Sorry I missed your reply to this.  I appreciate you sharing your insights into the design.

I have re-read the extensions documentation with fresh eyes and have a bit better understanding of the motivation for the activation mechanism.  

Part of my concern was it felt like we were introducing shared state into the protocol and that worried me.  However, on re-read, I believe the intent is for the A2A-extension header to be sent on every request, so if that is the case, then that concern is mitigated.

I do think there is value in the A2A-extension header for required extensions.  I think it is important that the A2A server reject the request if an extension is required and a client does not support it.  The only way for a server to know a client supports something is if the client declares support for it.

I think there may also be a valid use-case for an extension to be defined as opt-in.  This would require sending the header but the extension would be optional.  This could handle the case where a data extension returns a bunch of noisy data that isn't always needed. e.g. verbose-debug-info

I do think that there are some data extensions that a server would want to enable by default, even if a client does not explicitly ask.  I think if A2A extensions end up being popular like OpenAPI x- properties, then would be fairly burdensome for a client to have to enumerate all the extensions that the A2A server supports.  My guess is that devs will write generic code to walk the extensions advertized in the AgentCard and just auto generate the extension header.  At that point there is no explicit selection and we are just wasting bytes over the wire.

This phrase in the documentation

> Extensions should default to being inactive. This provides a "default to baseline" experience, where extension-unaware clients are not burdened by the details and data provided by an extension.

and this one are at odds with each other

> Not all extensions are activatable: data-only extensions exist solely to provide additional information via an AgentCard. Clients may still request activation of these extensions. Since the extension does not perform any additional logic upon activation, this should have no impact on the request.

If we introduce the notion of "opt-in" extensions, then it would require an additional config boolean in the extension definition. But with that we could say something like:

> When one or more extensions are required for correct operation, A2A clients MUST include the A2A-Extensions header field in requests to A2A servers. This header field MUST contain a list of absolute URLs identifying the required extensions. 

> If no extensions are required, the A2A-Extensions header field MAY be omitted.
Upon receiving a request, an A2A server MUST verify that all required extensions are declared in the A2A-Extensions header field. If any required extension is missing or the header field is absent when required, the server MUST reject the request. 

> A2A servers MAY activate non-required extensions regardless of client declaration. Alternatively, a server MAY choose to activate an extension only if the client explicitly opts in by listing the extension in the A2A-Extensions header field.

And regarding the "x-" prefix on the header, I really think we should drop that.  It sends the "we don't care about industry standard specifications" message to some people. Also, if we do want to register it in the [IANA registry](https://www.iana.org/assignments/http-fields/http-fields.xhtml) it will be much easier to do without the x-.

I do think the header is the right way to communicate this information though.']

['Hey @vishalmysore — fantastic breakdown! I really appreciate how your RBAC implementation directly supports differential skill exposure, and how cleanly it integrates with security annotations.

That said, I’ve been reflecting on how this aligns with the intent behind the `authenticatedExtendedCard` endpoint as defined in the A2A spec. The spec seems to suggest a fairly strong separation of responsibilities:

- `.well-known/agent.json` should serve as a **stable, unauthenticated discovery surface**
- `/agent/authenticatedExtendedCard` is the designated place for **role/permission-aware views**
- Clients are expected to cache and *replace* the public card with the authenticated one for the session

So while your approach to filtering the public agent card is powerful and very usable, I wonder if it might be worth **keeping `.well-known/agent.json` stable and minimal**, and focusing RBAC-based expansion on the `authenticatedExtendedCard` path — especially to preserve compatibility with tools that assume `.well-known` remains open. You could still hint at extended views by including a flag like:

```json
"supportsAuthenticatedExtendedCard": true
```

in the public card, letting clients know they can elevate their context.

That said, I fully agree with the broader direction: enabling RBAC/ABAC-aware Agent Card responses is a key enabler for richer, context-sensitive capabilities — and the design you've laid out fits well with that vision. I think this will become even more interesting when the spec offers more concrete guidance around authentication and authorization flows — at that point, revisiting this kind of implementation detail could help us align even more closely and build interoperable solutions.

Would love your thoughts on whether this separation makes sense from your implementation perspective!', "@herczyn @Tehsmash 

This is how I am thinking about the discovery flow:

- All clients will discover the agent URL from an Agent Card.

- If a signature is present in the Agent Card, clients MUST verify the card contents against the signature before calling the Agent.

- If an Agent Card supports an extended card, the Agent SHOULD use the provided authentication details to access the Extended Card and use that information as a replacement for the original Agent Card used for discovery.

- The Extended Card MAY have additional information not included in the original card. That information MAY be specific to the authenticated caller.


I don't really have a problem with getExtendedCard just returning the same card as is publicly available if there is no extra information.  There may not be today, but there may be tomorrow.  e.g. an agent might add a skill but a client uses an older cached agent card.  When a an agent updates, it is going to take a some time for all registries to get updated with the latest version of the card.
Also, a client may choose to block access to an updated agent until a registry administrator approves access. 

I don't think we should be encouraging the sharing of direct Agent URLs, bypassing the Agent Card.  There are just too many variables between protocol bindings, security config,  protocol versions and all the security risks of bypassing card signatures.", 'Hey! Thanks for the great question. Currently, my implementation focuses on role-based filtering at the `/.well-known/agent.json` endpoint, as that's where most clients interact. While I haven't explicitly implemented the `/agent/authenticatedExtendedCard` endpoint yet, my current architecture demonstrates the core concepts that would make this extension natural and powerful. Here's my practical experience with role-based agent card filtering:

## Current Implementation

```java
@Agent(groupName = "car booking")
public class CarBookingAgent {
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('USER')")
    @Action(description = "Book a car")
    public String bookCar(/*...*/) { /*...*/ }

    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
    @Action(description = "Cancel a booking")
    public String cancelCarBooking(/*...*/) { /*...*/ }
}
```

In my current implementation, when different roles access the `/.well-known/agent.json` endpoint, they get different views of the agent's capabilities (which could be easily extended to the `/agent/authenticatedExtendedCard` endpoint):

1. **USER Role Gets:**
   ```json
   {
     "skills": [
       {
         "id": "bookCar",
         "name": "Book Car",
         "description": "Book a car for rental"
       },
       {
         "id": "getBookingStatus",
         "name": "Get Status",
         "description": "Check booking status"
       }
     ]
   }
   ```

2. **ADMIN Role Gets:**
   ```json
   {
     "skills": [
       // All USER skills plus:
       {
         "id": "cancelCarBooking",
         "name": "Cancel Booking",
         "description": "Cancel an existing booking"
       },
       {
         "id": "generateReport",
         "name": "Generate Report",
         "description": "Generate booking reports"
       }
     ]
   }
   ```

## Suggested Specification Enhancements

I think we could enhance the specification in these areas:

1. **Role-Based Skill Filtering**
   ```typescript
   interface AgentCard {
     // ...existing fields...
     securityContext?: {
       roleBasedFiltering: boolean;
       supportedRoles: string[];
     }
   }
   ```

2. **Skill-Level Security Metadata**
   ```typescript
   interface Skill {
     // ...existing fields...
     security?: {
       requiredRoles: string[];
       requiredPermissions: string[];
     }
   }
   ```

3. **Version Tracking for Role-Specific Cards**
   ```typescript
   interface AgentCard {
     // ...existing fields...
     roleSpecificVersion?: string; // Version specific to role combination
   }
   ```

## Current Implementation & Future Extension

1. **Current Approach**
   ```java
   @GetMapping("/.well-known/agent.json")
   public AgentCard getAgentCard(Authentication auth) {
       Set<String> roles = (auth != null) 
           ? auth.getAuthorities()
               .stream()
               .map(GrantedAuthority::getAuthority)
               .collect(Collectors.toSet())
           : Collections.emptySet();
           
       return agentCardGenerator.generateForRoles(roles);
   }
   ```

2. **Planned Extension for authenticatedExtendedCard**
   - Will maintain MCP compatibility
   - Extend current role-based filtering
   - Add support for more detailed capability exposure
   - Implement proper caching with role-specific versions

3. **Performance Optimization**
   - Pre-compute common role combinations
   - Cache role-specific card versions
   - Use lazy loading for large skill sets

You can see a working example in my [A2A-MCP-Security demo](https://vishalmysore-a2amcpdemo.hf.space/index.html).

## Benefits of This Approach

1. **Clean Separation of Concerns**
   - Security logic stays in annotations
   - Card generation respects security context
   - Easy to maintain and extend

2. **Flexible Authorization**
   - Supports both RBAC and ABAC
   - Can handle complex permission combinations
   - Works with custom security rules

3. **Better Client Experience**
   - Clients see only what they can access
   - Reduced need for error handling
   - Clearer capability boundaries

While I haven't implemented the `/agent/authenticatedExtendedCard` endpoint yet (as current clients primarily use `/.well-known/agent.json`), I'm will be  evolving this implementation as client support grows. The suggested specification enhancements could work well for both endpoints while maintaining compatibility with the MCP specification.

Would love to hear thoughts on these suggestions and discuss how we can best support both A2A and MCP clients as they adopt authenticated RBAC based actions!']

